  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through Sunday)\nIssued at 1247 AM CDT Mon May 25 2026\n\nOn Tuesday, a large upper level trough will be located across the \nwestern CONUS. An upper level disturbance rotating around the base \nthe of the main upper level trough will approach West Central Texas \nTuesday into Tuesday night. As this feature approaches, large scale \nascent will overspread the area resulting in in the development of \nshowers and thunderstorms to our west by early to mid afternoon. \nThis activity will then likely merge into one or two clusters of \nthunderstorms and spread east through West Central Texas from late \nafternoon into the overnight hours. High (80-100%) PoPs are expected \nTuesday into Tuesday evening. A few strong to severe thunderstorms \nare possible, especially across our western counties. Locally heavy \nrainfall is also possible, which may result in flooding. Highs on \nTuesday will be in the mid to upper 80s. Showers and thunderstorms \nwill linger across the eastern half of the area Wednesday morning \nand possibly into the early afternoon hours. Mainly dry conditions \nwill return by Wednesday evening. Highs on Wednesday will be in the \nupper 70s to mid 80s. \n\nOn Thursday, a large upper level will strengthen and drift south \nacross the western CONUS. Meanwhile, a weak upper level trough will \nbe located across east Texas. This will result in weak upper level \nridging across the forecast area on Thursday. Highs will be in the \nmid to upper 80s, with mainly dry conditions prevailing. Friday into \nthe weekend, the upper level low to our west will lift north, while \nan upper level trough slowly approaches from the west. This will \nresult in low end chance PoPs beginning Friday and continuing into \nthe weekend. Highs for this time frame will be in the mid 80s to \nlower 90s.",
